instruction:
You are tasked with designing a module in Verilog that implements a 4-to-1 multiplexer (MUX) with two select inputs (S0 and S1) and four data inputs (A0, A1, A2, and A3). The output of the MUX should be assigned to the output port X. In addition, the module should have power and ground inputs (VPWR and VGND, respectively), as well as two power supply inputs (VPB and VNB) for power domain isolation.

You should use the following module template:

```
module mux4 (
    X   ,
    A0  ,
    A1  ,
    A2  ,
    A3  ,
    S0  ,
    S1  ,
    VPWR,
    VGND,
    VPB ,
    VNB
);

    // Module ports
    output X   ;
    input  A0  ;
    input  A1  ;
    input  A2  ;
    input  A3  ;
    input  S0  ;
    input  S1  ;
    input  VPWR;
    input  VGND;
    input  VPB ;
    input  VNB ;

    // Your code here

endmodule
```

Your module should be designed to meet the following requirements:

- The inputs and outputs should be declared as shown in the template.
- The module should implement a 4-to-1 MUX with two select inputs (S0 and S1) and four data inputs (A0, A1, A2, and A3).
- The output of the MUX should be assigned to the output port X.
- The module should have power and ground inputs (VPWR and VGND, respectively).
- The module should have two power supply inputs (VPB and VNB) for power domain isolation.

Your module should be designed to be synthesizable and meet the following constraints:

- The module should be implemented within 200 lines of Verilog code.
- The module should use only standard Verilog constructs and should not rely on any vendor-specific libraries or constructs.
- The module should be optimized for area and power consumption.

original_rtl:
module mux4 (
    X   ,
    A0  ,
    A1  ,
    A2  ,
    A3  ,
    S0  ,
    S1  ,
    VPWR,
    VGND,
    VPB ,
    VNB
);

    // Module ports
    output X   ;
    input  A0  ;
    input  A1  ;
    input  A2  ;
    input  A3  ;
    input  S0  ;
    input  S1  ;
    input  VPWR;
    input  VGND;
    input  VPB ;
    input  VNB ;

    // Local signals
    wire [3:0] data_in;
    wire [1:0] select;
    assign data_in = {A3, A2, A1, A0};
    assign select = {S1, S0};

    // MUX implementation
    assign X = data_in[select];

endmodule

